WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.250 --> 00:00:01.910
Imagine choosing the rules of

2
00:00:01.910 --> 00:00:04.547
society, without knowing whether you'll

3
00:00:04.547 --> 00:00:06.098
be rich or poor.

4
00:00:06.422 --> 00:00:08.004
That's John Rawls's veil of

5
00:00:08.004 --> 00:00:10.889
ignorance, from A Theory of

6
00:00:10.889 --> 00:00:13.029
Justice, 1971.

7
00:00:13.372 --> 00:00:15.305
First, equal basic liberties for

8
00:00:15.305 --> 00:00:16.469
all. Second,

9
00:00:16.469 --> 00:00:18.560
inequalities are allowed only if

10
00:00:18.560 --> 00:00:20.856
they benefit the least advantaged,

11
00:00:20.856 --> 00:00:23.546
with fair equality of opportunity.

12
00:00:23.905 --> 00:00:25.846
Robert Nozick argued that justice

13
00:00:25.846 --> 00:00:27.256
is about fair process,

14
00:00:27.256 --> 00:00:28.341
not patterns.

15
00:00:28.341 --> 00:00:31.140
Communitarians like Michael Sandel said

16
00:00:31.140 --> 00:00:33.365
Rawls ignored community.

17
00:00:33.720 --> 00:00:34.891
Your turn.

18
00:00:34.891 --> 00:00:37.448
When are inequalities allowed?
